($q$B$q$,$q$03d4a598-d027-449a-896a-205d982f12c5$q$::uuid,$q$A5$q$,$q$For Quiz A the z-score is (86-74)/8 = 12/8 = 1.5. For Quiz B I got (62-52)/4 = 10/4 = 2.0 so the student did better relative to the class on Quiz B since that z-score is higher. Z-scores are useful because they put different scores on a common scale by measuring how many standard deviations away from the mean each score is, so you can compare across different tests.$q$,$q$anthropic$q$,$q${"content_key":"APSTATS-SFRQ-002","answer_type":"A5","expected":[{"criterion_key":"a1","present":true},{"criterion_key":"b1","present":false},{"criterion_key":"c1","present":true},{"criterion_key":"d1","present":true}]}$q$::jsonb,$q$780e6163bae3778a7ed24045eea60180301e7f49cc9323b99b49c4400f8b77da$q$,$q$provisional_accept$q$,$q$accf82300d7abca7bed0f7bbc6a389da97190fada7caa7efe78f2c66626c0b5b$q$),
($q$B$q$,$q$03d4a598-d027-449a-896a-205d982f12c5$q$::uuid,$q$A6$q$,$q$The z-score for Quiz A is (86-74)/8 = 1.5. For Quiz B, the z-score is (62-52)/4 = 2.5. The student performed better relative to the class on Quiz B. Z-scores are useful for comparing how well someone did on different tests, especially when the tests have different means or ranges.$q$,$q$google$q$,$q${"content_key":"APSTATS-SFRQ-002","answer_type":"A6","expected":[{"criterion_key":"a1","present":true},{"criterion_key":"b1","present":true},{"criterion_key":"c1","present":true},{"criterion_key":"d1","present":false}]}$q$::jsonb,$q$5744d00374dc531b27fd9ba22f45819f410bed81f8e265771f4bdc2d2b2f3d4e$q$,$q$provisional_accept$q$,$q$accf82300d7abca7bed0f7bbc6a389da97190fada7caa7efe78f2c66626c0b5b$q$),
($q$B$q$,$q$03d4a598-d027-449a-896a-205d982f12c5$q$::uuid,$q$A7$q$,$q$So for Quiz A, z = (86 - 74)/8 = 12/8 = 1.5. For Quiz B, I think it’s (62 - 52)/4 = 10/4 = 2.25, so that’s higher relative to the mean. So she did better on Quiz B compared to her class, since z-scores just tell you how many standard deviations above or below the mean you are, which makes different tests comparable.$q$,$q$deepseek$q$,$q${"content_key":"APSTATS-SFRQ-002","answer_type":"A7","expected":[{"criterion_key":"a1","present":true},{"criterion_key":"b1","present":false},{"criterion_key":"c1","present":true},{"criterion_key":"d1","present":true}]}$q$::jsonb,$q$f30f9dbdfd0cc084f97ec559f9bda66eed09e41d46df67965784916f7a70c276$q$,$q$provisional_accept$q$,$q$accf82300d7abca7bed0f7bbc6a389da97190fada7caa7efe78f2c66626c0b5b$q$),
($q$B$q$,$q$b0bd8a7f-333e-45c8-8cf4-07a13fb6b4f4$q$::uuid,$q$A1$q$,$q$For each additional hour a student studies, their predicted test score increases by about 4.1 points on average. The association between study hours and test scores is strong and positive. A student studying for 6 hours would be predicted to score 76.6. If they actually scored 74, the residual is -2.6, meaning their observed score was 2.6 points lower than predicted.$q$,$q$google$q$,$q${"content_key":"APSTATS-SFRQ-003","answer_type":"A1","expected":[{"criterion_key":"a1","present":true},{"criterion_key":"b1","present":true},{"criterion_key":"c1","present":true},{"criterion_key":"d1","present":true}]}$q$::jsonb,$q$52fa7ee0f34e9d1de9e528ecee14a769643d8caa0f161004118fbae9d83aedd9$q$,$q$provisional_accept$q$,$q$c832b32bf29ca2bb5626ae588ca0d0f649b27e0fd3817d0dd912410c33746039$q$),
($q$B$q$,$q$b0bd8a7f-333e-45c8-8cf4-07a13fb6b4f4$q$::uuid,$q$A2$q$,$q$So if they add an hour of studying, the score tends to go up by roughly 4.1 points—like, on average. The scatterplot looked pretty tight and going up, so I’d say it’s a strong positive thing. Plugging in 6 gives 52 + 24.6 = 76.6, but the kid actually got a 74, so the residual is 74 - 76.6 = -2.6, meaning they did worse than the line expected by about two and a half points.$q$,$q$deepseek$q$,$q${"content_key":"APSTATS-SFRQ-003","answer_type":"A2","expected":[{"criterion_key":"a1","present":true},{"criterion_key":"b1","present":true},{"criterion_key":"c1","present":true},{"criterion_key":"d1","present":true}]}$q$::jsonb,$q$e47113fee0a85645e2e597affd256b1ff6fe77b80ee0151959f728d71a44e5a8$q$,$q$provisional_accept$q$,$q$c832b32bf29ca2bb5626ae588ca0d0f649b27e0fd3817d0dd912410c33746039$q$),
($q$B$q$,$q$b0bd8a7f-333e-45c8-8cf4-07a13fb6b4f4$q$::uuid,$q$A3$q$,$q$The slope means that for each additional hour a student studies, their test score goes up by about 4.1 points on average. The relationship is strong and positive since r = 0.93 which is close to 1. For a student who studied 6 hours, the predicted score would be 52 + 4.1(6) = 52 + 24.6 = 76.6.$q$,$q$anthropic$q$,$q${"content_key":"APSTATS-SFRQ-003","answer_type":"A3","expected":[{"criterion_key":"a1","present":true},{"criterion_key":"b1","present":true},{"criterion_key":"c1","present":true},{"criterion_key":"d1","present":false}]}$q$::jsonb,$q$c2e263f5e5d6d1db687f9f5e4b644853baaafe65f0ce404ec217e94222e71dc9$q$,$q$provisional_accept$q$,$q$c832b32bf29ca2bb5626ae588ca0d0f649b27e0fd3817d0dd912410c33746039$q$),
($q$B$q$,$q$b0bd8a7f-333e-45c8-8cf4-07a13fb6b4f4$q$::uuid,$q$A4$q$,$q$The association between study hours and test scores is strong and positive. For a student studying 6 hours, the predicted score would be 52 plus 4.1 times 6. If their actual score was 74, the residual is -2.6, meaning their score was 2.6 points lower than predicted.$q$,$q$google$q$,$q${"content_key":"APSTATS-SFRQ-003","answer_type":"A4","expected":[{"criterion_key":"a1","present":false},{"criterion_key":"b1","present":true},{"criterion_key":"c1","present":false},{"criterion_key":"d1","present":true}]}$q$::jsonb,$q$393c492eb4c0e404d85f83acb237d97c2614d30b23a00662be6bf95d24b12abf$q$,$q$reader_queue$q$,$q$c832b32bf29ca2bb5626ae588ca0d0f649b27e0fd3817d0dd912410c33746039$q$),
($q$B$q$,$q$b0bd8a7f-333e-45c8-8cf4-07a13fb6b4f4$q$::uuid,$q$A5$q$,$q$The slope means for every extra hour of studying, the predicted score goes up by about 4.1 points, so more studying is linked to higher scores. For 6 hours, the line gives 52 + 4.1(6) = 76.6, but the actual score was 74, so the residual is 74 - 76.6 = -2.6, meaning they scored lower than expected. The correlation is pretty high, so the association is pretty tight.$q$,$q$deepseek$q$,$q${"content_key":"APSTATS-SFRQ-003","answer_type":"A5","expected":[{"criterion_key":"a1","present":true},{"criterion_key":"b1","present":false},{"criterion_key":"c1","present":true},{"criterion_key":"d1","present":true}]}$q$::jsonb,$q$4072bdbfcbd375c3322a87965b9802af2da26b9fa02ec325d7b1529760b8a830$q$,$q$reader_queue$q$,$q$c832b32bf29ca2bb5626ae588ca0d0f649b27e0fd3817d0dd912410c33746039$q$),
($q$B$q$,$q$b0bd8a7f-333e-45c8-8cf4-07a13fb6b4f4$q$::uuid,$q$A6$q$,$q$The slope means that for each extra hour of studying, the test score goes up by around 4.1 points. The correlation of 0.93 shows a strong positive relationship between study time and test scores. For 6 hours, the predicted score is 52 + 4.1(6) = 76.6. The residual would be 74 - 76.6 which is negative, so the actual score was a bit lower than what we'd expect from the regression line.$q$,$q$anthropic$q$,$q${"content_key":"APSTATS-SFRQ-003","answer_type":"A6","expected":[{"criterion_key":"a1","present":true},{"criterion_key":"b1","present":true},{"criterion_key":"c1","present":true},{"criterion_key":"d1","present":false}]}$q$::jsonb,$q$cd02ea5c54033736593a4f110941f46237fcecd49442c7d806bbdffdddc4d274$q$,$q$reader_queue$q$,$q$c832b32bf29ca2bb5626ae588ca0d0f649b27e0fd3817d0dd912410c33746039$q$),
($q$B$q$,$q$b0bd8a7f-333e-45c8-8cf4-07a13fb6b4f4$q$::uuid,$q$A7$q$,$q$For every additional hour a student studies, their predicted test score goes up by about 4.1 points, on average. The correlation is only like 0.43, but it's still a positive trend, so more study time means higher scores. If a student studied for 6 hours, their predicted score would be 52 + 4.1(6) = 76.6. Since their actual score was 74, the residual is 74 - 76.6 = -2.6, meaning their score was 2.6 points lower than the model expected.$q$,$q$google$q$,$q${"content_key":"APSTATS-SFRQ-003","answer_type":"A7","expected":[{"criterion_key":"a1","present":true},{"criterion_key":"b1","present":false},{"criterion_key":"c1","present":true},{"criterion_key":"d1","present":true}]}$q$::jsonb,$q$43ea01c8d816975ad76ebd493bcc8d7243e1e4e78770630a3521ca141af1e32e$q$,$q$provisional_accept$q$,$q$c832b32bf29ca2bb5626ae588ca0d0f649b27e0fd3817d0dd912410c33746039$q$),
($q$B$q$,$q$2e959510-8bab-4db6-8089-043329693934$q$::uuid,$q$A1$q$,$q$For the slope, each additional hour of screen time is associated with about 0.65 fewer hours of sleep on average. To predict for 7 hours I plug in: 9.8 - 0.65(7) = 9.8 - 4.55 = 5.25 hours of sleep. The residual is the actual minus predicted so 5.0 - 5.25 = -0.25 hours, which means this student slept less than what the model predicted. Using the line for 12 hours of screen time would be extrapolation because 12 is outside the observed range of 1 to 8 hours that the study actually collected data from.$q$,$q$anthropic$q$,$q${"content_key":"APSTATS-SFRQ-004","answer_type":"A1","expected":[{"criterion_key":"a1","present":true},{"criterion_key":"b1","present":true},{"criterion_key":"c1","present":true},{"criterion_key":"d1","present":true}]}$q$::jsonb,$q$0144e2e51631dbd6f76eaa5dcb62085b12536ea7f5e61c1710bbcc17d08399e1$q$,$q$provisional_accept$q$,$q$843d94157f650b12d7308565d497dd477b6a0d3e0ee69a6f67d977c563f304bf$q$),
($q$B$q$,$q$2e959510-8bab-4db6-8089-043329693934$q$::uuid,$q$A2$q$,$q$Like, each extra hour on a screen seems to drop sleep by about 0.65 hours. For someone with 7 hours screen time, the line says they'd get 5.25 hours of sleep, but this student only got 5.0, so they slept less than predicted, their residual was -0.25 hours. Trying to predict for 12 hours is extrapolation since the original study data was just for 1 to 8 hours, so 12 is way outside that range.$q$,$q$google$q$,$q${"content_key":"APSTATS-SFRQ-004","answer_type":"A2","expected":[{"criterion_key":"a1","present":true},{"criterion_key":"b1","present":true},{"criterion_key":"c1","present":true},{"criterion_key":"d1","present":true}]}$q$::jsonb,$q$75e99b6c3e28d55fcafbc5078b0207f74cdd66d532ed824ac2611172cef05cf7$q$,$q$provisional_accept$q$,$q$843d94157f650b12d7308565d497dd477b6a0d3e0ee69a6f67d977c563f304bf$q$),
($q$B$q$,$q$2e959510-8bab-4db6-8089-043329693934$q$::uuid,$q$A3$q$,$q$So like for every extra hour of screen time, sleep goes down by about 0.65 hours on average. For 7 hours, the line gives 9.8 - 0.65(7) = 5.25 hours. The residual is 5.0 - 5.25 = -0.25, so they slept less than predicted.$q$,$q$deepseek$q$,$q${"content_key":"APSTATS-SFRQ-004","answer_type":"A3","expected":[{"criterion_key":"a1","present":true},{"criterion_key":"b1","present":true},{"criterion_key":"c1","present":true},{"criterion_key":"d1","present":false}]}$q$::jsonb,$q$0f88e249893bda7b7c879408aec8f6df0bbee90da65c1bbd0358bcb604105296$q$,$q$provisional_accept$q$,$q$843d94157f650b12d7308565d497dd477b6a0d3e0ee69a6f67d977c563f304bf$q$),
($q$B$q$,$q$2e959510-8bab-4db6-8089-043329693934$q$::uuid,$q$A4$q$,$q$**(a)** For every additional hour of screen time, sleep decreases by 0.65 hours.

**(b)** Using the equation: 9.8 - 0.65(7) = 9.8 - 4.55 = 5.25 hours of sleep.

**(c)** The residual is 5.0 - 5.25 = -0.25 hours.

**(d)** This would be extrapolation because 12 hours is outside the observed range of screen time which only went from 1 to 8 hours, so we don't know if the relationship holds beyond that.$q$,$q$anthropic$q$,$q${"content_key":"APSTATS-SFRQ-004","answer_type":"A4","expected":[{"criterion_key":"a1","present":false},{"criterion_key":"b1","present":true},{"criterion_key":"c1","present":false},{"criterion_key":"d1","present":true}]}$q$::jsonb,$q$76c26286c940963836dd2d837e5ed958e80bbc96b24d0e44b24faf30f0347170$q$,$q$reader_queue$q$,$q$843d94157f650b12d7308565d497dd477b6a0d3e0ee69a6f67d977c563f304bf$q$),
($q$B$q$,$q$2e959510-8bab-4db6-8089-043329693934$q$::uuid,$q$A5$q$,$q$For each extra hour of screen time a student has, we'd expect their sleep to go down by about 0.65 hours on average. For 7 hours, the predicted sleep would be like, 9.8 + 0.65*7, which is 14.35 hours, but the student actually slept 5.0 hours, and if the predicted was 5.25, then the residual is 5.0 - 5.25 = -0.25 hours, so they got less sleep than expected. Using 12 hours would be extrapolation because the original data only goes from 1 to 8 hours for screen time, so 12 is way outside that range.$q$,$q$google$q$,$q${"content_key":"APSTATS-SFRQ-004","answer_type":"A5","expected":[{"criterion_key":"a1","present":true},{"criterion_key":"b1","present":false},{"criterion_key":"c1","present":true},{"criterion_key":"d1","present":true}]}$q$::jsonb,$q$f29c446cf870369a4ce590df8bec53be837cce9ec4710797cdfa6e046ffe6937$q$,$q$provisional_accept$q$,$q$843d94157f650b12d7308565d497dd477b6a0d3e0ee69a6f67d977c563f304bf$q$),
($q$B$q$,$q$2e959510-8bab-4db6-8089-043329693934$q$::uuid,$q$A6$q$,$q$The slope means that for every extra hour of screen time, you get about 0.65 fewer hours of sleep on average. So for 7 hours, the predicted sleep is 9.8 - 0.65(7) = 5.25 hours. Since the student actually slept 5.0, the residual is 5.0 - 5.25 = -0.25, so they slept a little less than what the model predicted. But for 12 hours, we don't have data that far out, so it's not safe to use the line.$q$,$q$deepseek$q$,$q${"content_key":"APSTATS-SFRQ-004","answer_type":"A6","expected":[{"criterion_key":"a1","present":true},{"criterion_key":"b1","present":true},{"criterion_key":"c1","present":true},{"criterion_key":"d1","present":false}]}$q$::jsonb,$q$921a15ab248060519edba03d0d1663d55b954d317d8a811bc4369ccc420e086f$q$,$q$reader_queue$q$,$q$843d94157f650b12d7308565d497dd477b6a0d3e0ee69a6f67d977c563f304bf$q$),
($q$B$q$,$q$2e959510-8bab-4db6-8089-043329693934$q$::uuid,$q$A8$q$,$q$Every extra hour of screen time means about 0.65 fewer hours of sleep on average. For 7 hours, the prediction would be like, less than 6. The residual is just the actual minus predicted. You can't use 12 hours because it's just too big for the model to handle.$q$,$q$google$q$,$q${"content_key":"APSTATS-SFRQ-004","answer_type":"A8","expected":[{"criterion_key":"a1","present":true},{"criterion_key":"b1","present":false},{"criterion_key":"c1","present":false},{"criterion_key":"d1","present":false}]}$q$::jsonb,$q$d4843eabb90366e9216545364b64256ed9294510fb190f4090c71c76efd3a43a$q$,$q$provisional_accept$q$,$q$843d94157f650b12d7308565d497dd477b6a0d3e0ee69a6f67d977c563f304bf$q$),
($q$B$q$,$q$0838c753-d383-48c5-a22f-aab1672eae4f$q$::uuid,$q$A1$q$,$q$They just asked the first 50 kids who walked by and said yes at lunch, so it’s basically a convenience sample. That’s biased because you’re only getting people who are at the cafeteria and willing to stop, so you’re missing all the kids who eat elsewhere or don’t want to talk, which is undercoverage. To do it better, you’d get a list of every student and use a random number generator to pick 50 of them. Even then, since this was just whoever happened to be there and agreed, the results probably won’t represent the whole school’s opinion.$q$,$q$deepseek$q$,$q${"content_key":"APSTATS-SFRQ-005","answer_type":"A1","expected":[{"criterion_key":"a1","present":true},{"criterion_key":"b1","present":true},{"criterion_key":"c1","present":true},{"criterion_key":"d1","present":true}]}$q$::jsonb,$q$ee03d1b8b0e8815e5980f17c072ee359a2cb27eab26a1ec65896eccc226469f6$q$,$q$provisional_accept$q$,$q$f713c464df1606feb03a95a87a149d99ddfd943aa0105c1e64151a405ee610d7$q$),
($q$B$q$,$q$0838c753-d383-48c5-a22f-aab1672eae4f$q$::uuid,$q$A2$q$,$q$(a) This is basically a convenience sample since they're just grabbing whoever happens to walk by and is willing to stop and answer their questions.

(b) The bias here is that they're only getting people who are in the cafeteria during that lunch period and who actually want to participate - students who have a different lunch or who rush past won't be included, so it's a self-selection thing combined with only covering part of the population.

(c) They should get a complete roster of every student in the school, assign each one a number, then use a random number generator or table to pick 50 different numbers to determine which students get surveyed.

(d) You can't really generalize this to everyone because the way they picked people means the sample probably doesn't match the actual makeup of the whole school - like maybe morning people who want later start times are more likely to hang around at lunch or agree to answer, we don't know.$q$,$q$anthropic$q$,$q${"content_key":"APSTATS-SFRQ-005","answer_type":"A2","expected":[{"criterion_key":"a1","present":true},{"criterion_key":"b1","present":true},{"criterion_key":"c1","present":true},{"criterion_key":"d1","present":true}]}$q$::jsonb,$q$82b064155aff92a78757055a31c1bca78ed5606ba2cef671eea88ec6e5ca9f28$q$,$q$provisional_accept$q$,$q$f713c464df1606feb03a95a87a149d99ddfd943aa0105c1e64151a405ee610d7$q$),
($q$B$q$,$q$0838c753-d383-48c5-a22f-aab1672eae4f$q$::uuid,$q$A3$q$,$q$This is a convenience sample cuz they just got the first 50 ppl who agreed at lunch. There's probably a self-selection bias since only students who feel strongly about the start time would bother answering. To do an SRS you'd need a list of all students, give them numbers, then use like a random number generator to pick 50 unique numbers for the sample.$q$,$q$google$q$,$q${"content_key":"APSTATS-SFRQ-005","answer_type":"A3","expected":[{"criterion_key":"a1","present":true},{"criterion_key":"b1","present":true},{"criterion_key":"c1","present":true},{"criterion_key":"d1","present":false}]}$q$::jsonb,$q$6fe26ff3ab8d329e328f6b68f30d71de54b22a6b97e1a1eb41b521a51cccaf06$q$,$q$reader_queue$q$,$q$f713c464df1606feb03a95a87a149d99ddfd943aa0105c1e64151a405ee610d7$q$),
($q$B$q$,$q$0838c753-d383-48c5-a22f-aab1672eae4f$q$::uuid,$q$A4$q$,$q$They basically just asked people who were at lunch and willing to talk, so it's like only the opinions of kids who eat at that time and are friendly enough to stop. That's a problem because kids who don't go to lunch or are in a rush aren't included, so the sample might not represent the whole school. To fix it, you could assign every student a number and use a random generator to pick 50, but even then you'd have to be careful because the results only really apply to the students who actually got selected, not everyone else.$q$,$q$deepseek$q$,$q${"content_key":"APSTATS-SFRQ-005","answer_type":"A4","expected":[{"criterion_key":"a1","present":false},{"criterion_key":"b1","present":true},{"criterion_key":"c1","present":false},{"criterion_key":"d1","present":true}]}$q$::jsonb,$q$a348d0e11c751286117dc57fcffd2bf9b3150e008e03bea6d7eef36ddadc73aa$q$,$q$reader_queue$q$,$q$f713c464df1606feb03a95a87a149d99ddfd943aa0105c1e64151a405ee610d7$q$),
($q$B$q$,$q$0838c753-d383-48c5-a22f-aab1672eae4f$q$::uuid,$q$A5$q$,$q$The sampling method is convenience sample since they just asked whoever was available at lunch. For a simple random sample they should make a list of all students in the school and use a random number generator to pick 50 of them. The results shouldn't be generalized because students at lunch might have different schedules or preferences than the whole student body - like maybe students with later classes are more likely to be at lunch then, so the sample doesn't represent everyone.$q$,$q$anthropic$q$,$q${"content_key":"APSTATS-SFRQ-005","answer_type":"A5","expected":[{"criterion_key":"a1","present":true},{"criterion_key":"b1","present":false},{"criterion_key":"c1","present":true},{"criterion_key":"d1","present":true}]}$q$::jsonb,$q$540fc89f4495c47d216f50f2dd8bfe1b778a12df29b4c0b123f695e4320c6fd9$q$,$q$provisional_accept$q$,$q$f713c464df1606feb03a95a87a149d99ddfd943aa0105c1e64151a405ee610d7$q$),
($q$B$q$,$q$0838c753-d383-48c5-a22f-aab1672eae4f$q$::uuid,$q$A8$q$,$q$This is a convenience sample because they're just asking whoever is nearby and willing to answer. Actually wait, I think it might be voluntary response since students have to agree to participate, so it's probably that instead. Or maybe it's both? I'm not really sure which one it technically counts as.$q$,$q$anthropic$q$,$q${"content_key":"APSTATS-SFRQ-005","answer_type":"A8","expected":[{"criterion_key":"a1","present":true},{"criterion_key":"b1","present":false},{"criterion_key":"c1","present":false},{"criterion_key":"d1","present":false}]}$q$::jsonb,$q$6075a40aaf5180a5cc7b163556ebe01c80cefafe5092297eb41fc13b957be187$q$,$q$provisional_accept$q$,$q$f713c464df1606feb03a95a87a149d99ddfd943aa0105c1e64151a405ee610d7$q$),
($q$B$q$,$q$6b092324-ceeb-4541-93ee-509ca8bf6a62$q$::uuid,$q$A1$q$,$q$The explanatory variable is whether students use the mindfulness app and the response variable is their quiz anxiety after. Random assignment is super important so the groups are pretty much equal on other stuff, which reduces confounding variables, and that way we can actually say the app caused any change in anxiety. For blocking, they should group students who have similar baseline anxiety scores together, like all the high anxiety people in one block, and then randomize who gets the app *within* those blocks. This is definitely an experiment because the researcher is giving out the treatment, the app, to the students.$q$,$q$google$q$,$q${"content_key":"APSTATS-SFRQ-006","answer_type":"A1","expected":[{"criterion_key":"a1","present":true},{"criterion_key":"b1","present":true},{"criterion_key":"c1","present":true},{"criterion_key":"d1","present":true}]}$q$::jsonb,$q$bcb9100d6a5f831db8a9919381709e8c6b540acb88d0b1d1070cf6423415b562$q$,$q$provisional_accept$q$,$q$1821d8705800b33f347499d559d8fb18936a3ebf4e4b04014260a96e3e45d5f1$q$),
($q$B$q$,$q$6b092324-ceeb-4541-93ee-509ca8bf6a62$q$::uuid,$q$A2$q$,$q$So the app usage is the explanatory thing, and the quiz anxiety after the 10 minutes is what we’re measuring. Random assignment is key because otherwise people who are already calm might choose the app, and we couldn’t tell if the app or their calmness caused the drop—it controls for lurking variables so we can say cause and effect. For blocking, we’d sort all 60 by their baseline anxiety scores, then make groups of similar scores (like low, medium, high) and randomly assign half of each group to the app and half to no app. It’s an experiment, not observational, because the researcher is actively deciding who gets the treatment.$q$,$q$deepseek$q$,$q${"content_key":"APSTATS-SFRQ-006","answer_type":"A2","expected":[{"criterion_key":"a1","present":true},{"criterion_key":"b1","present":true},{"criterion_key":"c1","present":true},{"criterion_key":"d1","present":true}]}$q$::jsonb,$q$ebe3da2ad8270af6b92481db23d1f2e105f1f40adac3571f5a9fa0bac8aacce0$q$,$q$provisional_accept$q$,$q$1821d8705800b33f347499d559d8fb18936a3ebf4e4b04014260a96e3e45d5f1$q$),
($q$B$q$,$q$6b092324-ceeb-4541-93ee-509ca8bf6a62$q$::uuid,$q$A3$q$,$q$The explanatory variable is whether students use the mindfulness app or not, and the response variable is their quiz anxiety level after the treatment. Random assignment is important because it helps balance out other factors that could affect anxiety between the groups so we can say the app actually caused any difference we see. For blocking, you could group students who have similar baseline anxiety scores together - like put all the high-anxiety students in one block, medium in another, low in another - and then randomly assign within each block.$q$,$q$anthropic$q$,$q${"content_key":"APSTATS-SFRQ-006","answer_type":"A3","expected":[{"criterion_key":"a1","present":true},{"criterion_key":"b1","present":true},{"criterion_key":"c1","present":true},{"criterion_key":"d1","present":false}]}$q$::jsonb,$q$445ac5e61b0e27c41f50973eb5f196e5bb752fa575ddb1ba07e360ce3c99e74e$q$,$q$provisional_accept$q$,$q$1821d8705800b33f347499d559d8fb18936a3ebf4e4b04014260a96e3e45d5f1$q$),
($q$B$q$,$q$6b092324-ceeb-4541-93ee-509ca8bf6a62$q$::uuid,$q$A4$q$,$q$The explanatory variable is what kind of intervention the students receive, and the response is their quiz anxiety after it. Random assignment is key because it helps make sure the groups are balanced, so we can be more confident that any difference is from the app and not some other factor, letting us find cause and effect. Blocks could be made up of students with similar baseline anxiety scores, for example, separating those with very high anxiety from those with lower anxiety. This is definitely an experiment because the researcher is the one deciding who gets what treatment.$q$,$q$google$q$,$q${"content_key":"APSTATS-SFRQ-006","answer_type":"A4","expected":[{"criterion_key":"a1","present":false},{"criterion_key":"b1","present":true},{"criterion_key":"c1","present":false},{"criterion_key":"d1","present":true}]}$q$::jsonb,$q$b8123461c2b3fffedd2d0cc1645266b8ae1266b6324d1b5069f1ba09d6103d94$q$,$q$reader_queue$q$,$q$1821d8705800b33f347499d559d8fb18936a3ebf4e4b04014260a96e3e45d5f1$q$),
($q$B$q$,$q$6b092324-ceeb-4541-93ee-509ca8bf6a62$q$::uuid,$q$A5$q$,$q$The explanatory variable is whether they used the app or not, and the response is their quiz anxiety score after the 10 minutes. Random assignment is needed so the groups are basically the same to start with. For blocks, you could split the volunteers into low, medium, and high baseline anxiety groups, then randomly assign half of each group to the app. This is an experiment because the researcher is giving the treatment to the students.$q$,$q$deepseek$q$,$q${"content_key":"APSTATS-SFRQ-006","answer_type":"A5","expected":[{"criterion_key":"a1","present":true},{"criterion_key":"b1","present":false},{"criterion_key":"c1","present":true},{"criterion_key":"d1","present":true}]}$q$::jsonb,$q$20ec37ea17afb7a97aacaea876250c06d77102c9ff59edbda0d8b096d9fbe914$q$,$q$provisional_accept$q$,$q$1821d8705800b33f347499d559d8fb18936a3ebf4e4b04014260a96e3e45d5f1$q$),
($q$B$q$,$q$6b092324-ceeb-4541-93ee-509ca8bf6a62$q$::uuid,$q$A7$q$,$q$The explanatory variable is using the mindfulness app, and the response variable is the student's quiz anxiety after the study. Random assignment is needed so that the groups are generally balanced and comparable before the treatment starts. For blocking, we could sort the 60 volunteers by their initial anxiety scores. Then, make 4 blocks of 10 students each, like highest anxiety, then next highest, and so on. Within each of these 4 blocks, we'd randomly assign half to use the app and half to a control group. This is an experiment because the researcher decides which students get the mindfulness app.$q$,$q$google$q$,$q${"content_key":"APSTATS-SFRQ-006","answer_type":"A7","expected":[{"criterion_key":"a1","present":true},{"criterion_key":"b1","present":false},{"criterion_key":"c1","present":true},{"criterion_key":"d1","present":true}]}$q$::jsonb,$q$28e98959be59f05b31922bc0df0ed5dae662a013ce70afe4dd43723cf162a960$q$,$q$provisional_accept$q$,$q$1821d8705800b33f347499d559d8fb18936a3ebf4e4b04014260a96e3e45d5f1$q$)
  returning gold_set_answer_id
)
